[Music]  welcome back so we're talking about the  SVD and one of the most important things  you need to know when you're using the  singular value decomposition for  dimensionality reduction of a data  matrix X is how your data is aligned  okay so in that example of eigenfaces  where we take each column of X is a  picture of a human face that's been  reshaped into a column vector it was  critical that all of those images were  cropped and aligned so that the same  pixels the same rows of ax meant the  same thing from image to image so that  image 1 this pixel it means an eye pixel  even an image 2 and 3 and fourths the  same meaning across all of the pixels  because the SVD is based on correlations  of the columns and of the rows and so if  I take the dot product of two columns  and the mouth is over here in one image  and over here in another then the dot  product doesn't really mean anything  then closeness of those two vectors  doesn't mean anything and so oftentimes  we're going to use this SVD for  dimensionality reduction in image  classification or image compression and  in that case you really do need to have  some kind of alignment of your data  features even more importantly when you  have when you're using this for physics  modeling like maybe this is data from a  simulation of a physical system so  columns are the state of a physical  system as they evolve in time maybe I  have a traveling wave or a rotating wave  or something like that the SVD is really  going to struggle to find a low-rank  representation of a traveling or  rotating structure because it's not  aligned in terms of the rows and the  columns of the data matrix okay and so  the examples that I have here are cooked  up to walk you through kind of what can  go wrong if you have a low rank matrix  and you rotate it or translate it or  something like that okay and again all  this code is at databook u-dub comm this  is the MATLAB version and so the first  code what I'm going to do is essentially  just cook up a large example  of two data matrices this is literally  just a data matrix that has zeros and  ones so black and white  this one has low rank structure so if I  take the SVD of this matrix it is almost  entirely characterized by one singular  value one extremely energetic singular  value and everything else is below the  the noise floor kind of machine  precision noise because this data is  aligned there is essentially one eigen  row and one eigen column that describe  that you can multiply together to make  this entire matrix okay essentially you  would you would literally just multiply  you know that that column by that row  and what you would get out would be this  matrix okay so it's really a 1 Rank 1  matrix  you know zeros and ones with rank 1 but  if you even rotate this a little bit if  you rotate this a little bit then the  the same kind of row and column  information from before gets destroyed  so that's what we're seeing over here on  the right is just a slightly rotated  version of this matrix where now you can  see that you actually have a very very  high rank effective SVD so when you look  at the singular values these are the the  logs of the diagonal elements of Sigma  these singular values take a long time  to decay it's over 200 rank system 250  degrees of freedom are needed to add up  to equal this matrix  even though as humans we know that  nothing really changed this is still a  rank 1 object in a rotated frame but the  SVD can't figure that out unless you  align that data into a frame where  everything kind of clicks into place  ok and it's you can make kind of a nice  visualization out of this where you just  rotate through increasingly large angles  here so this is another code in the book  where you basically take that same idea  and you just rotate it more and more and  more and what you can see in rainbow  colors here so as you go from yeah  - orange - blue - bright blue the rank  just gets worse and worse and worse as  you take this thing out of alignment in  in rows and column spaces so again the  SVD is not magic it's based entirely on  correlations between the columns of X  and and each other and the rows of X and  each other and so if you take something  low rank and kind of rotate it so that  those that alignment disappears in row  and column space you're gonna get this  rank explosion and so this is really  important if you have data similarly if  I took data of a square moving across  the screen so I had a movie of a square  moving across a screen or if my data had  you know something moving across the  columns even if it was the same object  the SVD would still give you a very high  rank system because it doesn't know  about translational invariance or  rotational invariance even though as  humans were really good at detecting  these things and recognizing that these  are all just squares at different  rotation angles okay so something to be  aware of when you use the SVD it can be  really really useful if rows and columns  mean the same thing from data set to  data set so as a kind of a counter  example of how what can go wrong if we  go back to that ovarian cancer data set  where we had 216 people and something  like 4,000 genetic markers that's a  great example of the data being aligned  because it was the same genetic marker  column 500 was the same genetic marker  across all of the people and column 501  was the same other genetic marker and so  the data was somehow aligned so that you  know columns meant the same thing across  all of the patients all of the people  okay so this is a cautionary tale you  have to be careful when doing the SVD  and make sure that your data is kind of  aligned and cropped and and organized so  that you expect that there to be there  there will be these kind of row wise and  column wise correlations okay thank you

